          <a href="#brightener" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. The Brightener You Also Need</a>
          <a href="#examples" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Three Worked Examples</a>
          <a href="#buying" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Buying and Leftovers</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Running out of stripper halfway through a section is worse than running out of paint. The treated area dries, the softened finish sets back into the grain harder than it was, and that patch becomes the most difficult part of the whole deck.
        </p>

        <div id="coverage-rates" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Coverage Rates, and Why They Vary So Much
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck stripper coverage is usually quoted somewhere between 100 and 200 square feet per gallon, which is a wide enough range to be nearly useless on its own. The spread is real, and it is driven by three things: what finish you are removing, how porous and weathered the wood is, and whether the product is a thin liquid or a gel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finish type is the dominant variable. A worn semi-transparent stain sits mostly in the wood and needs only enough product to wet the surface and stay wet. A solid stain is a film that has to be broken through, which needs a heavier application. Deck paint needs a heavier application again, plus a gel so it does not dry before it has worked.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Finish Being Removed</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Practical Coverage</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Applications</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Effective per Gallon</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Worn semi-transparent stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">175-200 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~185 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Recent semi-transparent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150-175 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~160 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Semi-solid stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">125-150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~100 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain, one coat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-125 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~80 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solid stain, multiple coats</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~50 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deck paint</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">75-100 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~45 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Read the right-hand column, because that is the one to plan from. It combines the per-application rate with the realistic number of applications, and for a solid-stained or painted deck it is roughly half the headline figure. Planning from the number on the front of the bottle is the single most common reason people make a second trip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wood condition shifts it further. Old, grey, weathered softwood with open grain drinks stripper noticeably faster than tight, recently finished wood. Pressure-treated pine that has been down fifteen years can be at the thirsty end of every range. A hardwood like ipe is at the other end, which partly offsets how stubborn its finishes are.
          </p>

          <p>
            And gels cover less per gallon by design, because the point of a gel is a thicker film that stays wet. That is a feature rather than waste: on a painted deck a gel at 90 square feet per gallon will outperform a thin liquid at 150 because the thin one dries before it finishes working.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="measuring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Measuring the Real Surface, Not the Floor
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where the bigger error lives. People measure the deck the way an estate agent would, in floor area, and then buy stripper for a deck that has a great deal more surface than that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start with the floor: length times width. A 16 by 20 foot deck is 320 square feet. That part is easy and it is usually only about 60 percent of what you will treat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then add the railings, which is the number nobody calculates. A railing has two faces of top rail, two faces of bottom rail, a post on each end, and a spindle every four inches or so with four faces each. As a working shortcut that avoids measuring every baluster, allow roughly 3 square feet of treatable surface per linear foot of railing. A deck with 40 feet of railing therefore adds about 120 square feet, which on the example above is more than a third again on top of the floor.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Element</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Shortcut Rule</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Example</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Deck floor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Length x width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">16 x 20 = 320 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Railing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 sq ft per linear foot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40 ft = 120 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stairs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 sq ft per step, per foot of width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 steps x 4 ft = 32 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stair railing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 sq ft per linear foot</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 ft = 40 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fascia / skirt board</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Height x perimeter run</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 ft x 40 ft = 40 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under-deck framing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually not treated</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That example deck comes to 552 square feet of treatable surface against a 320 square foot floor. Anyone who measured the floor and bought accordingly is 42 percent short before the first application, which is exactly the margin that produces a panicked second trip on a Saturday afternoon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two refinements. Railings with wide flat balusters or a solid infill panel run higher than 3 square feet per foot; simple thin metal balusters run lower, because you are only treating the wood. And decide up front whether you are doing the fascia and the stair risers, because they are easy to overlook in the measurement and impossible to skip once the rest is stripped and they are not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not subtract for the area under a bench or a planter unless you genuinely intend to leave it. You will almost always decide to do it once the rest of the deck is clean and the contrast is visible.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concentrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Concentrate or Ready-to-Use: Do the Arithmetic Carefully
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck strippers come in both forms and comparing prices between them requires a step people routinely skip.
          </p>

          <p>
            A concentrate is diluted with water at a stated ratio, so a gallon of concentrate may produce two or more gallons of working solution. The coverage figures quoted on a concentrate are almost always for the mixed product, not the bottle, so a gallon of concentrate covering 150 square feet per mixed gallon at a two-to-one dilution effectively treats 300 square feet. Compare cost per treated square foot rather than cost per bottle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Powder concentrates, which are common with percarbonate strippers, are the most economical per square foot and the easiest to store. They also let you mix exactly what you need for each section, which matters because mixed stripper has a working life and does not keep well once made up. On a large deck done over two weekends, a powder means the second weekend starts with fresh product.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ready-to-use liquids and gels cost more per square foot and are worth it in two situations: small decks where the convenience outweighs the premium, and paint or heavy solid stain removal where a properly formulated gel is doing something a home-diluted concentrate cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the dilution ratio against the job. Many concentrates give a range, a weaker mix for light stain and a stronger one for solid finishes. Mixing at the weak end to stretch the product on a solid-stained deck is a false economy that produces a patchy result and a second application, consuming more in total than mixing correctly would have.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Where the Product Goes</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~40%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Surface that is not floor</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Solid stain vs semi-transparent</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Applications on paint or heavy solid</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1:1</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Brightener to stripper, roughly</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="applications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Many Applications to Budget For
          </h2>

          <p>
            The honest planning assumption depends entirely on what is on the deck, and it is better to over-buy than to discover this mid-job.
          </p>

          <p>
            One application is realistic for a weathered semi-transparent stain that is already failing. These strip readily and a single properly-dwelt application usually clears them, with perhaps a spot treatment on a sheltered area that kept its finish better.
          </p>

          <p>
            One to two for a semi-solid or a single coat of solid stain. Plan for one, buy for one and a half, and treat the second round as spot work on whatever remains rather than a full recoat of the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two for multiple coats of solid stain or any paint. This is not pessimism, it is the normal case, and the second application is generally lighter and more targeted than the first. Areas that were shaded or under a roof overhang will hold their film better than the sun-baked centre of the deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add a genuine contingency on top. Ten to fifteen percent over your calculated figure costs relatively little and removes the failure mode where a section dries partway through. Given that a dried-out section becomes harder to strip than it was originally, the contingency is cheaper than the alternative in both money and time.
          </p>

          <p>
            One planning note that saves product: do the test patch described in our solid stain stripping guide before buying in bulk. Ten minutes on a hidden square foot tells you the coat count and therefore which row of the table above applies to you, which is worth more than any rule of thumb on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brightener" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Brightener You Also Need to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Budgeting for stripper alone is the other common shortfall, because after a caustic strip the brightener is not optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Caustic strippers leave the wood strongly alkaline and visibly darkened. Alkaline wood accepts stain unevenly and can react with it, and the darkening does not weather away. An acid brightener, usually oxalic or citric acid based, neutralises the surface and restores the colour. This is chemistry rather than cosmetics, and skipping it shows up directly in the new finish.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget brightener at roughly the same treated area as the stripper, so if you need four gallons of working stripper solution, plan on about four gallons of working brightener. Brightener is almost always sold as a concentrate or powder and is considerably cheaper per square foot than stripper, so this adds less to the bill than it sounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brightener coverage is generally quoted higher than stripper, often 200 to 300 square feet per mixed gallon, because it is a rinse-and-neutralise step rather than a dwell-and-scrape one. Use the treated-surface figure you calculated for the stripper and apply the brightener's own coverage rate to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note that a brightener is a different product from a deck cleaner, and the two are frequently confused on the shelf because they sit together and both promise a cleaner deck. A cleaner is typically alkaline and removes dirt and mildew; a brightener is acidic and neutralises. Our guide on deck brightener versus deck cleaner explains why using one in place of the other undoes the step.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using a mild percarbonate stripper rather than a caustic one, the brightener step is less critical but still worth doing, because percarbonate is also alkaline and the acid rinse still evens out the colour before staining.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="examples" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Three Worked Examples
          </h2>

          <p>
            The method applied end to end.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 1: Small Deck, Weathered Semi-Transparent</h3>

          <p>
            A 12 by 14 foot deck, 168 square feet of floor, with 26 feet of simple railing adding about 78 square feet, and three steps 4 feet wide adding roughly 24. Total treated surface is 270 square feet. A worn semi-transparent stain at about 185 effective square feet per gallon needs 1.5 gallons, plus 15 percent contingency, so buy 2 gallons of working solution. Brightener at 250 square feet per gallon needs about 1.1 gallons, so buy enough concentrate for 1.5.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 2: Mid-Size Deck, One Coat of Solid Stain</h3>

          <p>
            A 16 by 20 foot deck, 320 square feet of floor, 40 feet of railing adding 120, four steps 4 feet wide adding 32, stair railing adding 40, and a fascia adding 40. Total treated surface is 552 square feet. One coat of solid stain at roughly 80 effective square feet per gallon needs 6.9 gallons; add contingency and buy 8 gallons of working solution. Brightener needs about 2.5 gallons.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 3: Large Deck, Painted</h3>

          <p>
            A 20 by 24 foot deck, 480 square feet of floor, 60 feet of railing adding 180, stairs and stair railing adding 90, fascia adding 50. Total treated surface is 800 square feet. Deck paint at roughly 45 effective square feet per gallon across two applications needs 17.8 gallons; with contingency, 20 gallons of working solution. This is the number that makes people reconsider the project, and it is why the test patch and the strip-versus-replace decision matter so much on a painted deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Notice the pattern across the three. The floor area roughly triples from the first example to the third, but the stripper requirement goes up more than tenfold, because the finish type compounds with the area. Two decks of the same size with different finishes are genuinely different purchases.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Buying, Mixing and What to Do With Leftovers
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few decisions at the point of purchase that save money or trouble.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy it all in one go, and round up rather than down. The cost of an extra container is small; the cost of running out mid-section is a dried patch that is harder to strip than the original finish, plus a trip. On a large job the bulk sizes are substantially cheaper per square foot, so rounding up often costs very little.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mix only what you will use within the session. Made-up stripper solution has a limited working life and loses strength, particularly percarbonate products, which begin releasing their oxygen as soon as they are wetted. This is the practical argument for powder concentrates on a job spread over two weekends: you mix fresh each time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not dilute below the recommended ratio for your finish to stretch the product. A weak mix on a solid-stained deck gives patchy removal, forces a second full application, and uses more product in total than mixing correctly would have. If the range on the bottle offers a strong and a weak mix, match it to what is actually on your deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store unmixed concentrate properly and it keeps for a long time; store mixed solution and it does not. Powder keeps best. Label anything you keep with the product and date, because an unmarked container of caustic solution in a shed is a genuine hazard, not just a mystery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, plan the disposal before you start rather than after. Caustic runoff kills plants, etches aluminium and glass, and stains concrete and pavers, so cover shrubs with sheeting, wet and cover any hard surfaces below, and check local guidance on where rinse water may go. On a raised deck, decide where the runoff lands before the first application.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring the Floor and Forgetting the Railings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A railing adds roughly 3 square feet of treatable surface per linear foot once you count both rail faces, the posts and all four faces of every spindle. On a typical deck the railings, stairs and fascia together are around 40 percent of the total surface, which is exactly the margin that forces a second trip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the Coverage Figure on the Front of the Bottle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                That number assumes one application on a thin, worn semi-transparent stain. For a solid stain or paint the effective rate, once you account for a heavier application and a second round, is roughly half of it. Plan from effective coverage per gallon, not per application.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Diluting a Concentrate Weak to Stretch It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A weak mix on a solid-stained or painted deck gives patchy removal and forces a full second application, consuming more product overall than mixing correctly would have. If the bottle offers a dilution range, match the strong end to the finish you actually have.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting to Buy Brightener</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                After a caustic strip the wood is strongly alkaline and visibly darkened, and both affect how the new finish takes. Budget brightener for the same treated area as the stripper. It is cheaper per square foot and almost always sold as a concentrate, so it adds less to the bill than people expect.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mixing the Whole Batch on Day One of a Two-Weekend Job</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Made-up stripper solution loses strength, and percarbonate products begin releasing their oxygen as soon as they are wetted. Mix only what you will use in the session. This is the practical reason to prefer powder concentrates on any job you will not finish in a single go.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much deck stripper do I need for a 300 square foot deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Three hundred square feet is usually the floor only. Add roughly 3 square feet per linear foot of railing, 2 per step per foot of width, and the fascia, which typically brings the real treated surface closer to 480. On a worn semi-transparent stain that is about 2.5 gallons of working solution; on one coat of solid stain, about 6.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many square feet does a gallon of deck stripper cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Between 100 and 200 square feet per application, but plan from the effective rate that includes the number of applications you will actually need. That is roughly 185 for a worn semi-transparent stain, 80 for one coat of solid stain, and 45 for deck paint over two rounds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to measure the railings separately?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the most commonly skipped step. A railing has two faces of top and bottom rail, the posts, and four faces on every spindle. Allow about 3 square feet per linear foot as a shortcut, or 4 for a stair railing. On a typical deck this adds a third or more to the floor area.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is concentrate cheaper than ready-to-use deck stripper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Per treated square foot, usually considerably, especially powder concentrates. The comparison needs care because coverage figures for a concentrate normally refer to the mixed product, so a gallon of concentrate at a two-to-one dilution treats twice the quoted area. Compare cost per square foot treated, not per bottle.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much brightener do I need after stripping?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan for the same treated surface area as the stripper. Brightener coverage is usually higher, often 200 to 300 square feet per mixed gallon, and it is cheaper per square foot, so it adds modestly to the total. After a caustic strip it is a required step rather than an optional one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will one application of stripper be enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a weathered semi-transparent stain, usually yes. For one coat of solid stain, plan for one and buy for one and a half. For multiple coats of solid stain or any paint, two applications is the normal case, with the second being lighter spot work on the areas that held their film.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the type of wood change how much stripper I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, though less than the finish does. Old, grey, open-grained softwood drinks stripper faster than tight recently-finished wood, and fifteen-year-old pressure-treated pine sits at the thirsty end of every range. Dense hardwoods like ipe use less, which partly offsets how stubborn their finishes tend to be.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

